WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:11.120
"How Useful Is Personalized Nutrition?"

00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:16.320
Personalized nutrition is rooted in the
concept that one size does not fit all,

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:18.800
and who doesn't want
to think they're special?

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:23.360
The concept of personalized nutrition
is inherently appealing to the human ego.

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:27.200
Simple messages recognizing
individuality therefore resonate

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:28.640
deeply with consumers,

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:32.560
explaining the popularity of such
messages in sales and marketing,

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:35.200
even to the point of
manufacturing personalized food

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:38.400
for people's uniqueness,
suggesting 3D Food Printing

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:41.600
as a good candidate
for food customization.

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:45.360
Now, there certainly are some
legitimate differences between people.

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:48.800
Some have a peanut allergy and
keel over if they eat a peanut.

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:51.440
And others have celiac disease
and have to avoid gluten,

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:54.320
or genetically are lactose intolerant.

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:56.960
There's an enzyme mutation
common in some parts of Asia

00:00:56.960 --> 00:01:00.833
that protects against alcoholism
because people with it can't

00:01:00.833 --> 00:01:04.560
metabolize alcohol as efficiently, 
so toxic metabolites build up.

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:08.800
I did a fascinating video about fast
versus slow caffeine metabolizers

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:10.560
and the difference in health benefits.

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:13.520
That actually extends
to athletic performance.

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:17.040
Caffeine is ergogenic,
performance enhancing,

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:19.200
but only in fast metabolizers,

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:22.400
shaving more than a minute
off 10 kilometers of cycling,

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:24.960
whereas slower
metabolizers got no benefit,

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:27.680
or caffeine actually
slows them down,

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:31.440
adding two minutes to their cycling
time, depending on which kind of genes

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:35.200
they have that codes the
enzyme that breaks it down.

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:41.520
But for most people, in most situations,
we are more similar than different.

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:44.800
While there's undoubtedly a specific
minority of individuals who clearly

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:48.480
require a more personalized
approach to nutrition, there is presently

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:53.440
insufficient rationale for truly personalized
nutrition for the majority of people.

00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:57.200
Yet there is an astonishing number
of direct-to-consumer genetic testing

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:00.800
companies that have proliferated,
offering personalized nutrition advice,

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:04.320
for example, supplement-hawking
companies claiming to help consumers

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:09.360
optimize micronutrient status on the
basis of a handful of genetic variants.

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:12.560
But the majority of these variants
explain just a few percent

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:16.000
of the difference in levels
between individuals.

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:19.440
Personalized nutrition is part
of a broader push towards

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:22.960
"personalized medicine," also
known as "precision medicine"

00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:25.440
given the "massive cultural allure"

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:29.280
of personal control over prevention,
diagnosis, and treatment of disease,

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:34.320
stimulating demand and prompting
intense commercialization.

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:38.880
But unlike monogenetic diseases,
which are rare genetic diseases

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:43.680
caused by a single malfunctioning gene
like hemophilia or sickle cell anemia,

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:48.080
most diseases are caused by a complex
interaction between multiple genes

00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:52.320
with environmental factors, which pose
a major challenge for the realization

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:54.000
of personalized medicine.

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:56.480
Take something like
adult stature, for example.

00:02:56.480 --> 00:02:59.520
We've found at least 40 locations
on our chromosomes that have been

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:03.200
associated with human height,
which is strongly inherited.

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:06.480
The genes from your parents account
for about 80% of the difference

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:10.560
in height between people, yet all
those dozens of genes we've found

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:15.120
explain only about 5% of
height variation between people.

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:18.960
Researchers find those genetic links
by using what are called genome-wide

00:03:18.960 --> 00:03:21.440
association studies, where you
scan through all the chromosomes

00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:24.560
and look for statistical
associations between diseases

00:03:24.560 --> 00:03:28.400
and any particular stretches of DNA.
Okay, that's interesting,

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:31.920
but what companies marketing
genetic susceptibility tests are doing

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:37.520
is reinterpreting these data as if
they were predictive of individual risks.

00:03:37.520 --> 00:03:40.640
But all you're really getting are
modest genetic associations

00:03:40.640 --> 00:03:44.880
with a slight increase in the risk of
disease and with little predictive value

00:03:44.880 --> 00:03:47.920
when compared to more
significant contributions of things

00:03:47.920 --> 00:03:51.200
we already know
like lifestyle behaviors.

00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:55.200
Currently, the practice of utilizing
the DNA of an individual to predict

00:03:55.200 --> 00:04:01.520
disease has been judged to provide
little or no useful information.

00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:05.200
For example, let's say your genetic
analysis says you're at slightly greater

00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:09.120
risk for some grave condition compared
to others in your ancestral group.

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:13.040
This person was given advice
to exercise, keep their weight down,

00:04:13.040 --> 00:04:16.880
not drink too much alcohol, and eat
fruits, vegetables, and whole grains.

00:04:16.880 --> 00:04:20.560
Okay, solid advice,
but this is how we should live

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:22.800
regardless of our genetic risk.

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:26.880
And we all know, or should know,
these simple, bedrock strategies

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:30.560
to reduce the risk of common
chronic diseases in general.

00:04:30.560 --> 00:04:34.240
The problem, of course, is that
very few individuals live that way.

00:04:34.240 --> 00:04:37.760
Actually, to be more precise,
almost nobody lives that way.

00:04:37.760 --> 00:04:41.280
That's not just hyperbole.
Nationwide surveys show that

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:45.440
nearly the entire U.S. population
consumes a diet that is not on par

00:04:45.440 --> 00:04:48.640
with even the wimpy dietary
guidelines recommendations.

00:04:48.640 --> 00:04:53.920
In other words, almost no one in the
United States is eating a healthy diet.

00:04:53.920 --> 00:04:57.840
Studies like that remind us that from
the perspective of public health,

00:04:57.840 --> 00:05:00.400
worrying about personalizing
our preventive strategies

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:06.135
based on genetic risk information
borders on the absurd.

